SOLUTIONS = {'Multiply: 214 × 34': 'Step 1: Break 34 into 30 + 4.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 214 × 30 = 6,420 and 214 × 4 = 856.\n\nStep 3: Add: 6,420 + 856 = 7,276.\n\n214 × 34 = 7,276.', 'Multiply: 356 × 42': 'Step 1: Break 42 into 40 + 2.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 356 × 40 = 14,240 and 356 × 2 = 712.\n\nStep 3: Add: 14,240 + 712 = 14,952.\n\n356 × 42 = 14,952.', 'Multiply: 178 × 56': 'Step 1: Break 56 into 50 + 6.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 178 × 50 = 8,900 and 178 × 6 = 1,068.\n\nStep 3: Add: 8,900 + 1,068 = 9,968.\n\n178 × 56 = 9,968.', 'Multiply: 462 × 29': "Step 1: It's easier to multiply by 30 and then subtract one group of 462.\n\nStep 2: 462 × 30 = 13,860.\n\nStep 3: Subtract one 462: 13,860 − 462 = 13,398.\n\n462 × 29 = 13,398.", 'Multiply: 139 × 47': 'Step 1: Break 47 into 40 + 7.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 139 × 40 = 5,560 and 139 × 7 = 973.\n\nStep 3: Add: 5,560 + 973 = 6,533.\n\n139 × 47 = 6,533.', 'Multiply: 283 × 61': 'Step 1: Break 61 into 60 + 1.\n\nStep 2: Multiply: 283 × 60 = 16,980 and 283 × 1 = 283.\n\nStep 3: Add: 16,980 + 283 = 17,263.\n\n283 × 61 = 17,263.', "Write 'forty-two thousand, six hundred seventeen' in figures.": "Step 1: 'Forty-two thousand' = 42,000.\n\nStep 2: 'Six hundred seventeen' = 617.\n\nStep 3: Combine: 42,000 + 617 = 42,617.", "Write 'sixty thousand, nine' in figures.": "Step 1: 'Sixty thousand' = 60,000.\n\nStep 2: 'Nine' means only the ones place has a value — the hundreds and tens are 0.\n\nStep 3: Combine: 60,000 + 9 = 60,009.", 'How do you write 36,924 in words?': "Step 1: Split the number into groups: 36 (thousands) and 924 (ones group).\n\nStep 2: 36 = 'thirty-six', so the thousands part is 'thirty-six thousand.'\n\nStep 3: 924 = 'nine hundred twenty-four.'\n\nStep 4: Combine: 'thirty-six thousand, nine hundred twenty-four.'\n\nAnswer: A.", 'Find the missing number in the pattern: 17,340,  27,340,  ___,  47,340,  57,340': 'Step 1: Compare consecutive numbers: 27,340 − 17,340 = 10,000.\n\nStep 2: The pattern increases by 10,000 each time.\n\nStep 3: 27,340 + 10,000 = 37,340.\n\nThe missing number is 37,340.', 'Find the missing number in the pattern: ___,  32,150,  42,150,  52,150,  62,150': 'Step 1: Compare consecutive numbers: 42,150 − 32,150 = 10,000.\n\nStep 2: The pattern increases by 10,000 each time, so work backward from 32,150.\n\nStep 3: 32,150 − 10,000 = 22,150.\n\nThe missing number is 22,150.', 'What number goes in the blank?\n72,509 = 70,000 + ___ + 500 + 9': 'Step 1: Add up the parts you know: 70,000 + 500 + 9 = 70,509.\n\nStep 2: Subtract that from the total: 72,509 − 70,509 = 2,000.\n\nThe missing number is 2,000.', 'What number goes in the blank?\n84,027 = ___ + 27': 'Step 1: Subtract 27 from 84,027.\n\nStep 2: 84,027 − 27 = 84,000.\n\nThe missing number is 84,000.', 'In 63,281, what is the value of the digit 3?': 'Step 1: Write out the place values of 63,281.\n  6 = ten-thousands\n  3 = thousands\n  2 = hundreds\n  8 = tens\n  1 = ones\n\nStep 2: The digit 3 is in the thousands place, so its value is 3 × 1,000 = 3,000.', 'In 54,239, which digit is in the hundreds place?': 'Step 1: Write out the place values of 54,239.\n  5 = ten-thousands\n  4 = thousands\n  2 = hundreds\n  3 = tens\n  9 = ones\n\nStep 2: The hundreds digit is 2.\n\nAnswer: C (2).', 'Round $73,640 to the nearest $100.': "Step 1: Look at the tens digit in 73,640 — it's 4.\n\nStep 2: Since 4 is less than 5, round down (keep the hundreds digit the same).\n\nStep 3: 73,640 → 73,600.", 'What is the greatest factor of 54 that is less than 54?': 'Step 1: List the factors of 54: 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18, 27, 54.\n\nStep 2: The greatest one that is less than 54 is 27.\n\nAnswer: B (27).', 'Which of the following is NOT a factor of 42?': 'Step 1: List the factors of 42: 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 14, 21, 42.\n\nStep 2: Check each option — 6, 7, and 14 all appear in the list, but 8 does not.\n\nAnswer: C (8).', 'What is the smallest common multiple of 4 and 6?': 'Step 1: List multiples of 4: 4, 8, 12, 16...\n\nStep 2: List multiples of 6: 6, 12, 18...\n\nStep 3: The smallest number that appears in both lists is 12.', 'What is the second smallest common multiple of 8 and 12?': 'Step 1: List multiples of 8: 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48...\n\nStep 2: List multiples of 12: 12, 24, 36, 48...\n\nStep 3: The common multiples in order are 24, 48, 72... The second smallest is 48.', 'The product of two numbers is 195. One of the numbers is 13. What is the other number?': 'Step 1: If two numbers multiply to make the product, divide the product by the number you know.\n\nStep 2: 195 ÷ 13 = 15.\n\nThe other number is 15. Check: 13 × 15 = 195 ✓', 'The product of two numbers is 312. One of the numbers is 8. What is the other number?': 'Step 1: Divide the product by the number you know.\n\nStep 2: 312 ÷ 8 = 39.\n\nThe other number is 39. Check: 8 × 39 = 312 ✓', 'Jasmine saved $12,450. Marcus saved $9,000. How much more money did Jasmine save than Marcus?': "Step 1: Subtract Marcus's savings from Jasmine's.\n\nStep 2: $12,450 − $9,000 = $3,450.\n\nJasmine saved $3,450 more than Marcus.", 'A vendor had 42 boxes of oranges. There were 18 oranges in each box. If he sold all the oranges at 4 for $1, how much money did he receive?': 'Step 1: Find the total number of oranges.\n  42 × 18 = 756\n\nStep 2: Find how many groups of 4 oranges that makes.\n  756 ÷ 4 = 189\n\nThe vendor received $189.', 'A shopkeeper had 36 boxes of pears. There were 20 pears in each box. If she sold all the pears at 4 for $1, how much money did she receive?': 'Step 1: Find the total number of pears.\n  36 × 20 = 720\n\nStep 2: Find how many groups of 4 pears that makes.\n  720 ÷ 4 = 180\n\nThe shopkeeper received $180.', '1/3 + 1/3 = ?': 'Step 1: The denominators match, so add the numerators: 1 + 1 = 2.\n\nStep 2: Keep the denominator the same: 2/3.\n\n1/3 + 1/3 = 2/3.', '1/5 + 3/5 = ?': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so add the numerators: 1 + 3 = 4.\n\nStep 2: Keep the denominator: 4/5.\n\n1/5 + 3/5 = 4/5.', '2/7 + 4/7 = ?': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so add the numerators: 2 + 4 = 6.\n\nStep 2: Keep the denominator: 6/7.\n\n2/7 + 4/7 = 6/7.', '3/9 + 2/9 = ? Write the answer in simplest form.': "Step 1: Denominators match, so add the numerators: 3 + 2 = 5.\n\nStep 2: 5/9 — check if it can simplify. 5 and 9 share no common factor besides 1, so it's already in simplest form.\n\n3/9 + 2/9 = 5/9.", '4/10 + 3/10 = ? Write the answer in simplest form.': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so add the numerators: 4 + 3 = 7.\n\nStep 2: 7/10 cannot be simplified further.\n\n4/10 + 3/10 = 7/10.', '2/9 + 3/9 + 1/9 = ? Write the answer in simplest form.': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so add all the numerators: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6.\n\nStep 2: That gives 6/9. Simplify by dividing both parts by 3: 6÷3=2, 9÷3=3.\n\n2/9 + 3/9 + 1/9 = 2/3.', '1/4 + 1/8 = ?\n(Hint: rewrite 1/4 as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of 8 first.)': 'Step 1: The denominators are different, so rewrite 1/4 with a denominator of 8: 1/4 = 2/8.\n\nStep 2: Add: 2/8 + 1/8 = 3/8.\n\n1/4 + 1/8 = 3/8.', '3/10 + 1/5 = ?\n(Hint: rewrite 1/5 as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of 10 first.)': 'Step 1: Rewrite 1/5 with a denominator of 10: 1/5 = 2/10.\n\nStep 2: Add: 3/10 + 2/10 = 5/10.\n\nStep 3: Simplify: 5/10 = 1/2.\n\n3/10 + 1/5 = 1/2.', '1/9 + 1/3 = ?\n(Hint: rewrite 1/3 as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of 9 first.)': 'Step 1: Rewrite 1/3 with a denominator of 9: 1/3 = 3/9.\n\nStep 2: Add: 1/9 + 3/9 = 4/9.\n\n1/9 + 1/3 = 4/9.', '2/5 + 3/10 = ?\n(Hint: rewrite 2/5 as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of 10 first.)': 'Step 1: Rewrite 2/5 with a denominator of 10: 2/5 = 4/10.\n\nStep 2: Add: 4/10 + 3/10 = 7/10.\n\n2/5 + 3/10 = 7/10.', '1/2 + 1/6 = ?\n(Hint: rewrite 1/2 as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of 6 first.)': 'Step 1: Rewrite 1/2 with a denominator of 6: 1/2 = 3/6.\n\nStep 2: Add: 3/6 + 1/6 = 4/6.\n\nStep 3: Simplify: 4/6 = 2/3.\n\n1/2 + 1/6 = 2/3.', '5/7 − 2/7 = ?': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so subtract the numerators: 5 − 2 = 3.\n\nStep 2: Keep the denominator: 3/7.\n\n5/7 − 2/7 = 3/7.', '7/9 − 4/9 = ? Write the answer in simplest form.': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so subtract: 7 − 4 = 3, giving 3/9.\n\nStep 2: Simplify: divide both parts by 3: 3÷3=1, 9÷3=3.\n\n7/9 − 4/9 = 1/3.', '9/10 − 3/10 = ? Write the answer in simplest form.': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so subtract: 9 − 3 = 6, giving 6/10.\n\nStep 2: Simplify: divide both parts by 2: 6÷2=3, 10÷2=5.\n\n9/10 − 3/10 = 3/5.', '11/12 − 7/12 = ? Write the answer in simplest form.': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so subtract: 11 − 7 = 4, giving 4/12.\n\nStep 2: Simplify: divide both parts by 4: 4÷4=1, 12÷4=3.\n\n11/12 − 7/12 = 1/3.', '5/8 − 3/8 = ? Write the answer in simplest form.': 'Step 1: Denominators match, so subtract: 5 − 3 = 2, giving 2/8.\n\nStep 2: Simplify: divide both parts by 2: 2÷2=1, 8÷2=4.\n\n5/8 − 3/8 = 1/4.', '1 − 3/7 = ?': "Step 1: Think of 1 whole as 7/7 (same denominator as the fraction you're subtracting).\n\nStep 2: Subtract: 7/7 − 3/7 = 4/7.\n\n1 − 3/7 = 4/7."}

def fetch_grade4_questions(subject):
    url = f"{BASE}/questions?grade=eq.4&subject=eq.{subject}&select=id,text&limit=500"
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={"apikey": KEY, "Authorization": f"Bearer {KEY}"})
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as r:
        return json.loads(r.read())

def patch_solution(qid, solution):
    url = f"{BASE}/questions?id=eq.{qid}"
    data = json.dumps({"solution": solution}).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=data, method="PATCH", headers=HEADERS)
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as r:
        return r.status

print("Fetching grade 4 Math questions...")
questions = fetch_grade4_questions("Math")
print(f"Found {len(questions)} questions")

matched = 0
skipped = 0
for q in questions:
    text = q["text"]
    solution = SOLUTIONS.get(text)
    if solution:
        status = patch_solution(q["id"], solution)
        print(f"  \u2713 [{status}] {text[:60]}...")
        matched += 1
        time.sleep(0.1)
    else:
        skipped += 1

print(f"\nDone: {matched} updated, {skipped} not in this script's list")
